      "content": "No, the language \"without requiring prior written consent\" and \"applied automatically\" legally binds you to the 5% increase the moment the supplier declares the \"period,\" regardless of their claims about proving market volatility.\n\nThe phrase \"account for market volatility\" is likely just a strategic rationale the supplier will provide to justify the action, not a condition that requires their upfront proof or your agreement.\n\nYou will find it very difficult to claim this is a \"loophole\" for the board if I confirm the text explicitly overrides the need for your consent.\n\nI recommend framing the risk as a \"unilateral change in price terms\" to the board so they can demand a contract amendment that shifts the 5% surcharge to \"negotiated approval\" instead of \"automatic application.\"\n\nThis approach protects the deal by showing the contract is flawed on a critical risk, likely forcing the other party to agree to reasonable notice or a hard cap before you proceed.",
